states that the parent-first step is excluded from the trunk-freshness steps)\nTC-397 expected (all clauses) -> SR-246 AC / LLR-358 detail sentence for sentence (VERIFIED)\n\n## GAPS\n\n- Arm no TC clause states: TC-397 states none of SR-246's exemption-vs-refusal split for a missing registry by side (absent on the current side = no rows, no refusal; absent on the comparison side = every row moved, refused); its expected says only \"an ... absent spine registry ... receive no parent-first refusal\".\n- Arm no test exercises: SR-245 / LLR-357 \"a missing status reads no status\" in the rendered finding; P::test_a_parent_with_no_status_or_an_unknown_word_is_not_approved asserts only ids, and no test anywhere asserts \"no status\".\n- Arm no test exercises: SR-246 / LLR-358 an UNREADABLE comparison-side registry counts every status as moved; only the unparseable comparison side is tested.\n- Arm no test exercises: SR-246 / LLR-358 a lane commit compared with EVERY commit parent; the only lane-commit test is single-parent, so a first-parent-only judgement passes.\n- Arm no test exercises (weak, not absent): the exact \"the index: \" and \"commit <sha10>: \" prefixes on a finding line, and the full relationship text on any commit path, are never asserted.\n- Arm no test exercises (weak, not absent): a real child citing a -000 parent; and a B id in a parent column.\n- TC clause no row states: TC-397 method \"trunk-freshness exclusion\" (pinned by P::test_the_pre_commit_hook_runs_the_parent_first_step's `\"parent-first\" not in check._TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS`); no SR/LLR/IF sentence states it.\n- TC clause no row states (weak): TC-397 method \"the legacy carrier\" and \"hook\" rest only on LLR-358's \"first listed carrier\" and its module list.\n- Row claim the code contradicts: SR-246 \"compare a staged tree with ... every merge parent\" / LLR-358 \"against its current and merge parents\": acceptance_record.staged_parent_first_lines reads `rev-parse MERGE_HEAD^{commit}`, which yields only the first line of MERGE_HEAD; an octopus `git merge --no-commit a b` (confirmed in a scratch repo: MERGE_HEAD holds two shas, rev-parse returns the first) is not compared with its second merge parent. Low plausibility.\n- Row claim the code contradicts (wording): LLR-358 \"compares the first listed carrier for every CHANGED spine tier\": parent_first_lines reads all four tiers at both trees once any parent-first carrier changed, so an unchanged tier's unreadable/unparseable carrier refuses (current side) or marks every status moved (comparison side).\n- Row claim the code contradicts (reading-dependent): SR-246 \"leave ... a missing registry ... unrefused\": a registry missing on the comparison side and added with a violation is refused (G::test_a_registry_added_with_a_violation_is_refused); the SR does not say the exemption is current-side only.\n- Row claim the code contradicts (reading-dependent): SR-245 acceptance \"cites a ... declared SN, SR, or LLR parent\": read literally this covers a TC citing a Drafted SN, which spine.PARENT_EDGES and P::test_only_declared_tier_parents_are_judged deliberately do not judge; only LLR-357's edge list is precise.\n- Row claim the code contradicts (minor): SR-245 \"treat no other status as approved\": spine.claims_approval lower-cases the cell, so \"approved\"/\"FOUNDED\" (outside the closed vocabulary, flagged separately by enum_integrity_findings) count as approved and suppress a parent-first finding or refusal.\n\r\n[decision.D-009]\r\ndecided = \"The checkpoint reconcile (Terra, resumed) confirmed the coordinator's change list: TC-396 reads Full (its evidence includes tests in a SLOW_MODULES module), and SR-245 splits into two labelled derived SRs, SR-245 (the standing finding; LLR-357, TC-396) and the new SR-246 (commit admission; LLR-358, TC-397, IF-308's seam), with SR-245 and SR-246 both in the perspective record (--check --strict clean); the rows' text was checked against the settled code. Two coordinator edits follow, each a lane-added sentence no sitting has seen: the code's Implements tags for LLR-358's functions retagged SR-245 to SR-246 (the split's back-links, recipes section 5), and LLR-357's and LLR-358's details rewritten in contract voice without 'shall' (trace_text's form rule reserves 'shall' for the SR, and no other LLR uses it), the same content, by a guarded replace that asserted no other cell moved.\"\r\nalternative = \"Resume Terra for the two LLR details.\"\r\nreversal_cost = \"Low: Drafted rows before their sitting.\"\r\nwhy_not_escalated = \"A row split's retag and byte-level voice edits before the sitting that judges them.\"\r\nreview = \"\"\r\n### docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\r\n[requirement.SR-245]\r\ntitle = \"Standing approved-child cases are parent-first integrity findings\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-002\", \"SN-029\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-02\", \"B-09\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\"]\r\nrequirement = \"The delivered governance capability shall report every standing case in which an SR, LLR, or TC with Approved or Founded status cites a declared spine parent that lacks one of those statuses as one parent-first integrity finding that names the child and parent with both statuses, reads a missing status as no status, directs approval of the parent or return of the child to Drafted, and causes strict integrity to fail; treat no other status as approved; exclude IF, B, DA, undeclared, and -000 references from this judgement; and replace the former non-failing advisory for an Approved TC citing a Drafted LLR with that integrity finding.\"\r\nrationale = \"A DERIVED requirement, and labelled so. SN-002 requires mechanically verified traceability and SN-029 requires each approval to remain a review against the row's evidence, but neither states how a standing child approval must agree with its parent's approval. The consistency lens listens for related contracts that disagree: an approved child beneath an unapproved parent asserts a completed chain while its parent asserts the opposite. A standing integrity finding was selected over relying on a reader to compare four registries, because the approval vocabulary needs one visible meaning at the integrity boundary. Fed back to the needs: their acceptance could state that an approved trace chain contains no child whose cited parent remains unapproved.\"\r\nacceptance_criteria = \"Each Approved or Founded SR, LLR, or TC that cites a Drafted, missing-status, or unknown-status declared SN, SR, or LLR parent produces exactly one strict integrity finding beginning parent-first: and reading <child> (<status>) is approved under <parent> (<status>), which is not: approve <parent> in its own approval act, or return <child> to Drafted; a missing parent status reads no status, and strict integrity fails. A Founded parent produces no finding. IF, B, DA, undeclared, and -000 references produce no parent-first finding. No non-failing LLR-status advisory remains for an Approved TC citing a Drafted LLR.\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nverification = \"Test\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\nphase = 6\r\naspect = \"process\"\r\n\r\n[requirement.SR-246]\r\ntitle = \"A moved inconsistent approval relationship is refused at commit admission\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-002\", \"SN-029\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-02\", \"B-09\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\"]\r\n[requirement.SR-246]\r\ntitle = \"A moved inconsistent approval relationship is refused at commit admission\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-002\", \"SN-029\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-02\", \"B-09\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\"]\r\nrequirement = \"The delivered governance capability shall refuse a staged tree or lane commit that leaves an Approved or Founded child under a declared parent without either status when the child or parent status moved against any comparison parent, stating the parent-first relationship and prefixing the refusal with the staged-tree or abbreviated-commit identity; compare a staged tree with its current parent and every merge parent and a lane commit with every commit parent; treat an unreadable difference or current-side registry as a refusal and an unreadable or unparseable comparison-side registry as moving every status; and leave a simultaneous parent-and-child approval, a true first commit, a tree that changes no spine registry, a missing registry, and a commit that moved neither relevant status unrefused.\"\r\nrationale = \"A DERIVED requirement, and labelled so. SN-002 requires mechanically verified traceability and SN-029 requires each approval to remain a review against the row's evidence, but neither states how admission prevents a changed approval from creating an inconsistent chain. T
